+# Blocked: native async ratelimit is not available in the vendored library
+
+## What is blocked
+
+`Client.fetch_all_async()` cannot use a native async limiter, and
+`test_limiter_is_natively_async` / `test_fetch_all_async_uses_native_limiter`
+cannot pass, because the `RateLimiter` we import has no `acquire_async()`.
+
+## Why
+
+`vendor/ratelimit.py` is `ratelimit` 2.4.1 and exposes only:
+
+- `try_acquire()` (non-blocking)
+- `acquire()` (blocking, implemented with `time.sleep` / injected `sleep`)
+
+There is no `async def acquire_async` or any asyncio code in the module.
+`vendor/CHANGELOG.md` stops at 2.4.1 and does not mention async support, so
+the platform's "native async" release has not actually been synced into this
+repo. `vendor/` is synced from the platform repo and must not be hand-edited,
+so there is no supported way to add the API here.
+
+## What was done
+
+- Added `Client.fetch_all_async(urls)`, which awaits
+  `self.limiter.acquire_async()` before each request and awaits the fetch
+  coroutine, preserving input order.
+- Removed the `_ThreadedLimiter` workaround (and its `asyncio` import) from
+  `client.py`.
+
+The async client code is written against the API the platform described; it
+will work once the vendored library provides `acquire_async()`. Until then,
+the two async tests fail with `AttributeError`, and any real use of
+`fetch_all_async` with the current `RateLimiter` will fail the same way.
+`test_fetch_all_keeps_order` still passes.
+
+## What is needed to unblock
+
+Sync `vendor/` to the ratelimit release that includes native async support
+(or confirm its version), then re-run the tests.
